 35CL c  1966Ho02: a bremsstrahlung photon beam was produced by impinging       
 35CL2c  1.4-MeV electrons on a thin layer of lead embedded in a water-cooled   
 35CL3c  aluminium disc at the Diamond Research Laboratory in Johannesburg. The 
 35CL4c  target was C{-2}Cl{-6}. Scattered |g rays were detected using a 7.6-cm 
 35CL5c  by 7.6-cm NaI crystal. Deduced the width for the 1.22-MeV first excited
 35CL6c  state using the self-absorption method of nuclear resonance            
 35CL7c  fluorescence.                                                          
 35CL c  1962Bo17: an electron beam was produced from the MIT Van de Graaff     
 35CL2c  generator, passed through a 76-|mm aluminum window and a 25-|mm        
 35CL3c  platinum foil, and stopped in the cooling water of the electron target.
 35CL4c  |g rays were detected using a 5.1-cm by 5.1-cm NaI crystal. Lifetimes  
 35CL5c  of {+35}Cl levels are deduced from the measured intensity of the       
 35CL6c  resonance fluorescence radiation which occurs when a nucleus is        
 35CL7c  bombarded by bremsstrahlung.                                           
 35CL c  1991Li12: a bremsstrahlung photon beam was produced by focusing a      
 35CL2c  4.1-MeV electron beam onto a radiator target of a gold disc on a       
 35CL3c  water-cooled copper at the bremsstrahlung facility at the Stuttgart    
 35CL4c  Dynamitron Accelerator. The target was a stack of discs that contain   
 35CL5c  {+13}C, {+23}Na, {+27}Al, {+31}P, {+35}Cl, {+48}Ti. |g rays were      
 35CL6c  detected using 3 Ge detectors. Deduced |s of photon scattering.                            
